SUMMARY:How to Film the Life of an Electron (Dr Tom Siday)
LOCATION:Poynting Large Lecture Theatre\, Physics Building (R13)
DESCRIPTION:Dr Tom Siday\nInstitute of Physics Public Lecture Series\n\nEv
 erything around you — your phone\, solar panels\, computers\, the screen
  you're reading this on — works because of electrons zipping around insi
 de materials. But catching them in the act is absurdly hard: they move in 
 millionths of a billionth of a second\, across distances smaller than a si
 ngle atom. No ordinary camera comes close.In this talk\, I'll show how we 
 are building what amounts to the world's fastest and best-resolved camera\
 , using some of the shortest possible flashes of light to photograph matte
 r atom by atom at their native timescales. We're getting close to a long-s
 tanding dream: recording real-time movies of the fastest processes in natu
 re.\n\nDetails: https://www.birmingham.ac.uk/about/college-of-engineering-
 and-physical-sciences/physics-and-astronomy/outreach-and-schools-liaison/o
 utreach-for-secondary-schools/iop-public-lecture-series
